{"id": "P07183", "name": "Aydin Dogan", "name_chinese": "Aydin Dogan", "country": "Türkiye", "net_worth_usd_m": "1002.6", "net_worth_source": "Forbes real-time", "source_of_wealth": "Media", "industry": "media & entertainment", "sec_insider_companies": "", "shared_with": "", "appears_on": "Forbes real-time (2026); Forbes annual (2005-2025); Hurun Global (2015-2026)", "focus": [], "forbes_profile_url": "https://www.forbes.com/profile/aydin-dogan/", "wikidata_url": "", "forbes_bio": ["Aydin Dogan owns 29% of Dogan Holding, a public company active in oil distribution, electricity production, real estate, tourism and e-commerce.", "He was once Turkey's biggest media boss but came into conflict with the policies of the Turkish government.", "In 2018, he sold his portfolio (including TV channels Kanal D and CNN Turk, and the newspapers Hurriyet, Posta and Fanatik) for around $1.1 billion.", "In 2012, he entered into a licensing deal with Donald Trump for an office tower and shopping mall project in Instanbul.", "In 2019, he sold Trump Towers to publicly traded Dogan Holding for around $150 million."], "forbes_facts": ["In 2009, Dogan handed over management of the Dogan companies to his four daughters.", "In 2019, he brought out a book titled \"Ayd&#305;n Sozler\" (\"Enlightened Words\" - a pun on his name), consisting of aphorisms from his work life."]}
{"id": "P07295", "name": "Gary Friedman", "name_chinese": "Gary Friedman", "country": "United States", "net_worth_usd_m": "1000.0", "net_worth_source": "Hurun Global", "source_of_wealth": "RH", "industry": "Retail", "sec_insider_companies": "RH (RH) - CHAIRMAN & CEO", "shared_with": "", "appears_on": "Forbes real-time (former) (2026); Forbes annual (2021-2025); Hurun Global (2024-2026); SEC insider holdings (2026)", "focus": [], "forbes_profile_url": "https://www.forbes.com/profile/gary-friedman/", "wikidata_url": "", "forbes_bio": ["Gary Friedman is the CEO and chairman of publicly traded high-end furniture retailer RH, which generates over $3 billion in annual sales.", "He's credited with turning around the company formerly known as Restoration Hardware, which was at the brink of bankruptcy when he joined in 2001.", "The retail veteran started at Gap as a stock boy, eventually working his way up to managing 63 stores in southern California.", "He then spent 13 years at Williams-Sonoma Inc., where he took Pottery Barn from a $50 million tableware business to a $1 billion plus furniture brand.", "Friedman also helped conceptualize the West Elm brand and developed the Williams-Sonoma Grande Cuisine stores."], "forbes_facts": ["Friedman's dad died when he was 5 years old; he spent much of his childhood moving around with his mom, who had bipolar disorder and schizophrenia.", "After getting a D average during his first year at Santa Rosa Junior College in California, Friedman decided to drop out and focus on working at Gap."]}
